; 10-load.kurt

; Kurt's connectives beyond `implies`/`and` -- `or`, `not`, `iff`, and their
; inference rules -- are not hard-coded in Python.  They live in ordinary
; `.kurt` files under `src/kurt/theories/`, and `load` pulls one in.

load prop             ; propositional logic: or, not, iff, and-elim, ...

bool A, B
use A and B
A                      ; "and-elim" is now available, unlike in 04-and.kurt
B

; `load` looks in the current directory first, then in Kurt's own packaged
; theories -- so `load "my-theory.kurt"` also works for files you write
; yourself.  A file is only ever loaded once, even if several files (or the
; same file, twice) ask for it -- a second `load` just says so:

load prop             ; already loaded, skipped

; `load` with no arguments lists every file loaded so far:
load

; A symbol is declared by one file only: `+` comes from numbers.kurt, or from
; a structure with its own `+` (a field, a vector space), never from both --
; a theory's laws hold for everything written with its operators, and
; numbers.kurt's `$a * $b = $b * $a` must not reach matrices. Two files that
; declare the same symbol can't be loaded together (in either order); the
; same file reached twice is fine. Here, two small theories that both
; declare `∘` (written to files first, like your own theories would be):
;
;     one.kurt:   load equality / infix ∘ 50 50 / use $a ∘ $b = $b ∘ $a "comm"
;     two.kurt:   load equality / infix ∘ 50 50 / use $a ∘ $a = $a "idem"
;
; `load one, two` gives
;
;     EvalError: `∘` is declared in `one.kurt` and in `two.kurt` -- a symbol is
;     declared by one file only, so these two can't be loaded together
